{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"**The predicted stale-read exposure is now observed, not hypothetical.** Adding it as evidence for the \"Why this matters\" section.\n\nAt 2026-10-27 22:21 PDT, reading ticket `6b66-9378-ebb1-446c` through the two surfaces returned contradictory state:\n\n| | `status` | `plan_review_phase` | signature material |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| local CLI `rebar show` (main checkout) | **`open`** | `planning` | `file_impact 12`, description 4528 chars, `verified-at-sha 031be25ced9…` |\n| MCP `show_ticket` | **`closed`** | `execution` | `file_impact 14`, description 5816 chars, `verified-at-sha ee5253abfd4…` |\n\nThe MCP read is the correct one. The ticket had been closed minutes earlier, its `file_impact` widened twice, and its description edited — none of which the local CLI could see, because its `tickets` branch is the 32-commits-behind state this bug describes.\n\nThis matters beyond stale reads: the stale signature it returned belongs to a **superseded** plan review (material fingerprint `250926293c261b95`, from an earlier 12-path `file_impact`). An agent or script reading attestation state through the local CLI during this window would validate against a certificate that is no longer the ticket's current one. That is a correctness hazard, not just a freshness annoyance.\n\nIt also directly contradicts the premise that the local-CLI carve-out is safe for read-only checks. AGENTS.md routes `rebar review-plan <id> --status` and `rebar verify-commit-ticket` to the local CLI precisely because they are reads — but a read served from a 32-commit-stale branch can report `open` for a closed ticket and surface a retired attestation as current.\n\nStrengthens the final acceptance criterion already on this ticket: the question is not only whether the warning should become a hard failure, but whether **any** local-CLI read should be trusted while the branch cannot sync. A warning on stderr is not sufficient signal for a surface that agents are instructed to rely on."},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1793165648707002202,"uuid":"9101f600-02b2-4f16-ae2c-45521b6721ef"}